    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. we already tried mocking some of the external APIs and it didnt really help much, the timeouts are still happening randomly. it feels like something is messing with the container environment itself middle of the run. do u think it could be something like node contention or maybe a memory leak in the runner that only trips up occasionally? id like to know how to actually monitor the resource usage during the test phase to see if there's a spike when it fails.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Monitoring resource usage can indeed provide insight into potential issues like node contention or memory leaks. To do this, enable verbose logging or tracing within Jenkins to monitor thread and memory usage in real-time. Alternatively, tools like Prometheus and Grafana can be configured to collect metrics from the Jenkins master and agents during the execution of the test stage. A Java agent with detailed monitoring like JFR (Java Flight Recorder) or Mission Control can be attached to Jenkins to record detailed runtime data which can be analyzed afterwards. Also, consider adding a memory profiler to an integration run to detect leaks only when failures occur. If volunteering detail level on these metrics initially seems overwhelming, you could add instrumentation purposely to record specific events and resources, iteratively narrowing down the cause.",
