    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah prometheus/grafana sounds like a lot of setup for right now. is there anything simpler i can check in the docker logs or the agent itself? like, i'm just trying to figure out if it's a hardware thing or if some background process is just hijacking the CPU or something. it's so unpredictable, it almost feels like it's happening on purpose.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, you can take a less complex approach by reviewing the Jenkins agent logs directly through Docker commands. Retrieve the logs using the command `docker logs <container ID or NAME>` immediately after the build attempt. Look for patterns or errors related to high CPU usage or other resource issues. For a wider view, you can check the Jenkins agent monitoring section, which may provide insights into the resource distribution and usage over time. If you're suspecting a hardware issue, you could also compare RAM and CPU usage metrics of your nodes during the successful runs and the failing ones using tools like `htop` directly on the node machines if possible, or sysdig to capture more comprehensive baseline and errant behavior for analysis. While this approach is more rudimentary, it could help you to quickly identify anomalies in hardware or insufficient resources tied to your test failures.",
      "source": "target",
